  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 342 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nBlocked upper ridging remains over the central CONUS, with upper\ntroughing over the West Coast. This upper ridging is driving\nwell above average temperatures in the 70s currently over the \nregion. Weak embedded impulse moving through the flow is \nutilizing weak instability aloft, helping spark scattered \nrain/virga showers in northeast ND and northwest MN this \nafternoon into evening. Dry air in the lower levels will prevent\nmuch of this precipitation from reaching the ground, thus added\nsprinkles to the forecast to account for this.\n\nOn Friday, well above average temperatures will occur once more\nwith widespread 70s forecast during the day. While temperatures\nwill be mild, gusty southerly winds 20-40 mph will also build \nstarting Friday by late morning, lasting through the day.\n\nUpper trough in the West will attempt to break eastward,\nalthough all guidance suggests this will occur in a split-flow-\nlike regime, with the northern branch of the upper trough in\nnorthern CONUS and Canada separating from the southern branch\nlingering in the Southwest. As it does this, it brings\nsufficient moisture and lift to bring scattered showers Friday\nafternoon into evening. Most guidance also indicates at \nsufficient instability aloft to bring lightning/thunderstorms \ninto the conversation. A lack of better instability, moisture,\nand wind shear spacing will mitigate how strong thunderstorms\nwill be - likely keeping storms weak.\n\nStrong cold front moves through Friday night into Saturday\ndriven by the northern branch of the upper trough. This ends our\nwell above average temperatures, with most locations seeing\ndaily high temperatures Saturday occur just ahead of this cold \nfront, occurring early morning Saturday.\n\nThis strong cold front will also bring additional round of gusty\nwinds - this time out of the west. Winds have a medium to high\nchance in reaching advisory-criteria (ie sustained 30-40 mph, \ngusts 45-55 mph) in eastern ND west of the Red River Valley,\nwith slightly lower but still gusty winds elsewhere.\n\nTemperatures turn more average with highs in the 40s and 50s\nwith lows around freezing next week. An additional chance for\nlight rain comes around Monday, but confidence is low in this\ndue to disagreement in modeled guidance regarding whether or \nnot sufficient forcing for ascent will spread into our region. \nAt this time, light snow looks unlikely, but cannot be \ncompletely ruled out should forcing for ascent move through \novernight.",
